WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.040
Speaker 1: I don't want to spend a lot of
time on this.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.040
It's quite dense.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.129
It's worth reading on your own.

00:00:06.129 --> 00:00:10.670
But ProPublica did a really interesting study
of the richest, richest Americans.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:18.080
And what they looked at is how much in taxes
the richest Americans paid relative to how

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.759
much their wealth grew during the same time
period.

00:00:20.759 --> 00:00:23.810
Now, a lot of this has to do with unrealized
gains.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.349
And I'm going to explain that in a moment.

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:29.960
But this is one of the ways in which the rich
really benefit the super, super, super rich.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.360
I'm not talking about people who make five
hundred K a year.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:37.830
We're talking about the super super rich in
the United States.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:44.500
When you have an increase in net worth, this
could be because you earned money by working

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.960
or you might have an increase in net worth
because assets you hold increased in value.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.320
If you hold a bunch of stocks, a million dollars
in stocks and they go up and they're worth

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.490
two million dollars in stocks, your net worth
just went up by a million dollars.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:03.450
You don't owe any taxes other than on the
dividends, depending on what type of account

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.570
those funds are.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:05.570
Stocks are held then.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:06.570
Right.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.150
So but the gist of it is you don't owe any
taxes on that gain of a million dollars in

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:15.479
net worth until you realize the gain by selling
those stocks.

00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:22.700
So what you've seen over the last from 2014
to twenty eighteen is that the 25 richest

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:29.870
Americans saw their collective net worth go
up by 400 billion dollars, but they only paid

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:32.990
thirteen point six billion dollars in federal
income taxes.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.940
So that is a three point four percent.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.920
Now, I know many people are going to say,
David, David, hold on, they didn't have a

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.010
taxable event.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.170
A lot of that growth in.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.560
Networth came from they held assets and the
assets went up in value.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:52.090
I have a two million dollar house and the
market explodes and it's worth four million.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.020
My net worth went up to million dollars.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.060
I don't owe any taxes on that, OK?

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.070
My real estate taxes might go up a little
if the assessment goes up right.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.540
There's some details.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:01.540
That's how it works.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:02.540
David, I understand.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:08.019
But the analysis is an interesting one nonetheless,
which is when you were already very wealthy,

00:02:08.019 --> 00:02:14.879
you were able to increase your net worth with
very little tax exposure for the average person.

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:19.859
If you make 50000 dollars a year and you get
a raise to seventy five thousand dollars a

00:02:19.859 --> 00:02:21.700
year, that's a nice raise.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.980
That increase in the net worth you could have
by saving your additional salary is going

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.030
to be dragged down by the fact that you're
paying income taxes on that additional salary.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:39.349
So the investigation is an interesting one
because it exposes the tax on net worth growth,

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:41.450
which is really, really low.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:45.419
Now, am I proposing a particular change in
policy as a result of the.

00:02:45.419 --> 00:02:50.109
No, I have other segments on that, but it's
a very interesting way to look at how by not

00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:57.709
realizing gains and by having assets that
grow that are disproportionately already held

00:02:57.709 --> 00:03:05.120
by the very rich, you can have a net worth
grow with extremely low drag from taxes, tax

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:06.430
drag, as it is often called.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:10.730
And it is another one of the ways in which
just increasing income taxes, which is often

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:14.879
what the discussion is, it's what we're talking
about now with Joe Biden, does very, very

00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:20.709
little to actually capture the vast majority
of the benefit to the ultra, ultra ultra rich.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:23.219
We're talking about multibillionaires here.

00:03:23.219 --> 00:03:28.079
This is a very different thing than someone
with a small business like Joe the Plumber's,

00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:32.019
which supposedly made two hundred and fifty
thousand a year but didn't actually very,

00:03:32.019 --> 00:03:33.139
very different situation

